          <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;margin:0.75rem 0 0.4rem;">How does Wirral housing affect plumbing emergencies?</h3>
          <p>The Wirral peninsula has a remarkably varied housing stock. Birkenhead and Tranmere hold some of the oldest working-class terraces in the North West, built for Cammell Laird shipyard workers. Plumbing systems here have been patched and adapted over more than a century. Port Sunlight has around 900 Grade II listed homes. They have their own rules about what can and cannot be changed. Heswall, West Kirby and Neston have big detached homes with modern pressurised systems. Our engineers know all three ends of this spectrum. The <a href="https://www.hse.gov.uk/gas/domestic/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HSE gas safety guidance</a> applies the same to every home type.</p>
          <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;margin:0.75rem 0 0.4rem;">Does coastal air affect plumbing on the Wirral?</h3>
          <p>Yes, in a real way. The Wirral has two coasts. The Mersey side on the east and the Dee estuary and Irish Sea on the west. Homes on the coast see more rust than inland homes. That covers West Kirby, Hoylake, New Brighton, Neston and Parkgate. Outdoor pipes, overflows and metal fittings suffer most. Salt air off the Irish Sea and the Dee estuary speeds up the wear of outdoor parts. We carry marine-grade replacement fittings on every Wirral van as a result.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="copy-block">
          <h2>Finding a Reliable 24 Hour Plumber on the Wirral</h2>
          <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;margin:0.75rem 0 0.4rem;">What should I check before calling an emergency plumber on the Wirral?</h3>
